.TH CYAML_DESTROY 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cyaml_destroy \- recursively free a DOM tree and null the handle
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cyaml.h>

cyaml_destroy(node);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
When node is not NULL, this macro frees the whole DOM subtree under node
recursively, freeing each node with the allocator that the node stores,
and then sets node to NULL.

The macro frees the children of a list, the values of a dictionary and
the string copies, all recursively.  A second destroy of the same handle
is safe, because the macro sets the variable to NULL after it frees the
subtree.

For this reason node must be an assignable lvalue: you cannot give a
plain NULL literal or any other expression that is not an lvalue.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
node  cyaml variable to destroy.  The macro sets the variable itself
      to NULL.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro evaluates node exactly once, so an expression with a side
effect, such as handles[i++], destroys and clears one element.  node
must be a modifiable lvalue, such as a variable or an array element.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cyaml root = cyaml_parse("a: 1\en", NULL);
/* ... use root ... */
cyaml_destroy(root);   /* root is now NULL */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cyaml_declare_scoped (3)
